From 0ca17ad05c66fc231b6e84d2d3d5018d09da7881 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Matthias=20M=C3=BCnch?= <claude@djbase.de>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 06:17:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] bebbossh: convert UTF-8 <-> console codeset in
 interactive sessions

The server side talks UTF-8, the Amiga console uses the system codeset
(usually ISO-8859-1/-15). Interactive sessions (stdin AND stdout are a
console) now convert in both directions; redirected data stays untouched.

- Output: UTF-8 -> system codeset via codesets.library (optional, opened
  at runtime, V6), CSA_MapForeignChars for unmappable characters; a
  built-in ISO-8859-1 conversion is used if the library is missing.
  Sequences split across packets are buffered; invalid UTF-8 (e.g. from a
  Latin-1 server) is passed through unchanged. Window titles too.
- Input: typed characters (system codeset) -> UTF-8.
- LANG=C is only sent for non-interactive sessions.

Note: codesets.library 6.22 frees a caller-supplied CSA_Dest buffer when
CSA_MapForeignChars expands a character (crash) and reports a wrong
CSA_DestLenPtr, so the result buffer is allocated by the library.

Build: needs the codesets.library headers, e.g.
  make ... "C++FLAGS=-fno-exceptions -fno-rtti -I<libcodesets>/include"

Tested on Vampire V4SA (OS 3.2, codesets.library 6.22) against OpenSSH
10.0 with LANG=de_DE.UTF-8: umlauts, typographic quotes, dashes and
German system messages display correctly, typed umlauts arrive as UTF-8.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
